  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Structuring the Database**\n\nI\'ve decided to design the Room Database schema, now that I have the spec in front of me. I\'ve also begun by establishing a database package structure, `/app/src/main/java/com/example/data`. Now, I\'m identifying the necessary tables, starting with a `HabitEntity`.\n\n\n**Verifying Data Structures**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into the required data structures. I\'ve confirmed we need to use Room for local database persistence. So, I\'m setting up entities for `Habit`, `SufferingItem`, `DailyRun`, and `Profile`. I\'m starting to carefully verify their data structures, especially considering the dynamic habit weighting algorithm, which introduces further dependencies. I\'m now exploring the necessary calculations that will occur for the algorithm.\n\n\n**Interpreting the Formula**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the formula\'s variables. $M_t$ is straightforward - the mood recorded on day *t*.  I\'ve verified $H_{h,t-1}$ signifies habit *h*\'s completion on day *t-1*, impacting the mood on day *t*. I\'m also now digging into the provided markdown to see the missing variables and better understand the formula\'s visual representation.\n\n\n**Clarifying the Weight Formula**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the habit weight formula. The math rendering was off, but I think I\'ve reconstructed it. It seems we need to calculate a direct correlation. I\'m focusing on  $W_h = \\sum M_t \\cdot H_{h, t-1}$. I\'m also now considering adding a multiplicative factor of 5, which results in the equation of: $\\text{Weight} = \\sum_{t} \\text{Mood}(t) \\times \\text{Completed}(t-1) \\times 5$. And the card glows if the weight is $\\ge 0.5$.\n\n\n**Refining the Correlation Calculation**\n\nOkay, I\'ve refined the correlation calculation algorithm. Now, for each habit, I iterate through sorted daily runs, checking completion on the *previous* run\'s date to correlate with the *current* run\'s mood. I\'m focusing on the implementation details now: checking if completion occurred in the previous run. I\'m considering edge cases, especially the very first run.\n\n\n**Calculating the Weight**\n\nI\'m now implementing the weight calculation. I\'m focusing on the formula: if a `currentRun` has a mood *M* and habit completion *H*, I\'m adding *M* * H* to the sum. I also need to calculate the average weight based on a positive correlation.  I\'m also preparing to display the weight with an indication of its positive or negative value, and adding a visual cue for a positive score. It\'s smart to differentiate based on the score! I\'m now also looking into suffering inventory.\n\n\n**Calculating Suffering Value**\n\nI\'m now implementing the suffering item value calculation formula.  I\'ve double-checked the formula, ensuring the base values and daily coefficients are correct for each size: small, medium, and large. I\'m focusing on accurately representing the daily accumulation. I will also be sure to award the user Progress XP once they complete one of these items.\n\n\n\n**Defining the Schema**\n\nI\'m now defining the database schema. I\'ll create Room entities in `/app/src/main/java/com/example/data/model/Entities.kt`. The `Habit` entity includes an ID, name, description and `isDefault`. The `SufferingItem` will have an ID, name, size (\"small\", \"medium\", \"large\"), created date, completion status and `finalXpEarned`. I\'m starting the `DailyRun` Entity.\n\n\n**Simplifying the Daily Run**\n\nI\'ve decided on the structure for `DailyRun`. I\'ll store completed habit IDs as a comma-separated string, like `HabitIdsCsv`, and will use a `moodReported` field. Also, `sufferingConqueredCsv` will store suffering items. This approach is much cleaner than JSON. I\'m verifying the implications of \"daily reset\" \342\200\223 I will have to ensure that there\'s a fresh run created for each day at midnight local time.\n\nI am also defining the `Profile` entity!\n\n\n**Implementing the Streak**\n\nI\'m now implementing the logic for the user\'s streak. I will update the streak in the `Profile` entity. I realize I need to consider edge cases: for example, the very first login, and also, I must ensure that when the user has not logged in, we do not reset the streak. I need to make sure that it\'s updated only when there is user interaction! Also, I will need to consider the impact of the morning check-in. The streak should only increase if a run is associated with it. I will keep in mind I should also show a visual cue.\n\n\n\n**Defining the Streak**\n\nI\'m now implementing the streak logic with a focus on a simple check. If I log in today (T) and yesterday (T-1) had a run, I maintain the streak; otherwise, it resets to 1. Completing one habit or checking in daily keeps the streak alive.  The retro pixel art aesthetic idea is exciting, I\'m thinking about how to integrate that!\n\n\n**Designing the Sprite**\n\nI\'m now thinking about creating the `PixelArt` component, and I\'ve started defining a sprite using an array of hex color strings within Jetpack Compose. I\'m imagining using a custom `Canvas` to draw a crisp 8x8 or 16x16 grid of these colors as squares for the renderer!\n\n\n\n**Designing the Sprite**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in designing the retro sprites! I can render pristine 8-bit visuals with no asset files, perfectly scaled! I\'ve visualized the Hero, Heart, Sword, Shield, Triforce, and Habit icons. I can\'t wait to sketch them and generate the final pixel-art templates. This is fantastic!\n\n```\n\n```\n\n**Visualizing the Hero**\n\nI\'m now determining the optimal size, and settling on 16x16. I\'ve calculated the size in dp: it perfectly fits list items, buttons, or headers at 32dp. I\'ve also defined an NES Zelda color palette. Finally, I\'ve drafted a sprite grid, modeling Link, assigning colors to characters such as \'X\', \'k\', \'g\', \'d\', \'p\', \'w\', etc.\n\n\n**Refining the Hero Sprite**\n\nI\'m now iterating on my hero sprite, making it a 10x8 grid. I\'ve defined a dynamic builder to parse and convert each character. I\'m assigning each character in the pixel pattern to a specific color! I\'m now testing the pixel colors, with a focus on representing Link. This will make it easier to add it into Compose!\n\n```kotlin\nval HeroSpritePattern = listOf(\n    \"         \",\n    \"   YY   \",\n    \"  GGYY  \",\n    \"  GGyy  \",\n    \"  GGyy  \",\n    \"  BBbB  \",\n    \" XBBBBX \",\n    \" XBBBBX \"\n)\n```\n\nI\'m starting to think about additional sprites.\n\n\n**Generating the Sprite**\n\nI\'m now generating sprites with the defined character mapping, including Link\'s sword. I\'m focusing on rendering a pixel grid from character arrays. I\'ve designed the sword and started working on the shield, implementing the color map. I\'m focusing on ensuring these icons render beautifully!\n\n```kotlin\nval HeroSprite = listOf(\n    \"    KKKK    \",\n    \"   KGGGGK   \",\n    \"   KGGGGK   \",\n    \"  KGGGGGGK  \",\n    \" KGPKPPKPGK \",\n    \" KPPPPPPPPK \",\n    \"  KPKKKKPK  \",\n    \"  KPYWWYPK  \",\n    \"  KPPPPPPK  \",\n    \"   KGGGGK   \",\n    \"  KGGGGGGK  \",\n    \" KGGYGGYGGK \",\n    \" KGGGGGGGGK \",\n    \"  KBBKKBBK  \",\n    \"   KK  KK   \"\n)\n```\n\n```kotlin\nval SwordSprite = listOf(\n    \"      K     \",\n    \"     KWK    \",\n    \"    KWKK    \",\n    \"   KWKK     \",\n    \"  KWKK      \",\n    \" KWKK       \",\n    \"KWWK       \",\n    \" KBBK       \",\n    \"  BBB       \"\n)\n```\n\n\n**Refining the Sprite Icons**\n\nI\'m now iterating on additional sprites: a shield, a treasure chest, and a water potion for habit icons. I\'ve designed a red-blue-gold crest shield for medium worries, and a brown and gold treasure chest for large worries. The pixel-art aesthetics are coming together nicely, and I\'m looking at implementing a potion for the habit icons.\n\n\n**Implementing the Sprite**\n\nI\'ve completed designing several more sprites: boots for physical habits and a scroll for journaling. I\'m excited about the custom pixel art: I will now focus on building a `PixelSprite` composable that can render these sprites dynamically, taking either a named sprite or a custom pattern. Next, I need to focus on implementing the database with Room. I\'m focusing on defining the database helper functions. I will start by focusing on the directory structure.\n\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will create the file `/app/src/main/java/com/example/data/Entities.kt` which contains the Room Database entity annotations and models matching our JSON schema, and supporting our mathematical formulas (Daily Run, Habits, Suffering Inventory, and Profile stats)."
