  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Design Elements**\n\nI\'m currently dissecting the Design HTML to extract key components for the \"Immersive UI\" theme. I\'ve identified primary colors like `--nes-red` and `--nes-blue`. Now, I\'m focusing on translating these color codes and design principles from the HTML into actionable steps for the `MainLayout.kt` file.\n\n\n**Extracting Visual Concepts**\n\nI\'ve gathered additional color codes: `--nes-green`, `--nes-gold`, `--nes-black`, and `--nes-white`. I\'m now focused on understanding the HTML\'s aesthetic highlights. Specifically, I\'m analyzing the structure of the header, the hero sprite positioning, and the footer\'s game controller buttons with their corresponding labels. I\'m taking notes on their styles.\n\n\n**Integrating HUD Aesthetics**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in redesigning `HeaderStatsBar`. The goal is a perfect replica of the HTML\'s HUD. I\'m focusing on the left-side stats, specifically \"LIFE\" with dynamically rendered hearts based on streak, and retro resource counters for XP, keys, and habits. On the right, I\'ll calculate and display the level and run map name. The HTML provides a clear visual target!\n\n\n**Designing Console Interactions**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in the bottom bar, focusing on a retro physical console layout. I\'m prioritizing the SELECT, START, A, and B buttons, aiming to map their functionality directly to interactions within the panels. For instance, SELECT will cycle through the Tavern, Closet, and Morgue panels. The \"B\" button is tied to inventory modifications. The \"A\" button will give item status, help, and check.\n\n\n**Refining Button Functionality**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the console buttons\' roles. The START button will trigger the \"Morning Check-In\" flow or the game start sound. The A and B buttons will be red and circular. B will open an \"add\" dialog, adding habits in the Tavern or worries in the Closet. A will toggle habits or provide instructions. I am really focusing on making these interactions feel realistic and responsive.\n\n\n**Implementing HUD Display**\n\nI\'m now focused on the HTML to refine `HeaderStatsBar` in Jetpack Compose. I\'m building out the \"LIFE\" hearts, showing up to four based on the `streak` count. I\'m also adding the visual display for `gems/rupees`, `key items`, `active irritations` count and the `level` calculation. I\'m meticulously implementing the specific HTML structure, now that the compilation succeeded! I am focused on clean and precise Compose code.\n\n\n**Visualizing Heart Logic**\n\nI\'m now implementing the heart display within `HeaderStatsBar`. I\'m using a `maxHearts` variable set to four. I\'m dynamically rendering red hearts based on the `streak`, up to the `maxHearts`. Any streak exceeding four shows four hearts with a plus sign. I\'m also addressing the empty heart logic with black hearts.\n\n\n**Refining Heart Display**\n\nI\'ve just implemented the heart display logic in `HeaderStatsBar` with red and black hearts. Now, I\'m integrating the gem/rupee visual and XP counters. I am adding a \"\360\237\224\267\" symbol to represent the gems/rupees. The XP counter will be paired with the symbol. I am using `NES_WHITE` color and bold font style to ensure readability.\n\n\n**Adding Item Visuals**\n\nI\'m now integrating visuals for key items and active suffering within `HeaderStatsBar`. I\'m incorporating a \"\360\237\227\235\357\270\217\" symbol for keys and \"\360\237\222\243\" for active suffering, displaying counts next to them. I\'m leveraging monospace font and bold style in `NES_WHITE` to ensure a consistent retro aesthetic.\n\n\n**Calculating Level Progression**\n\nI\'m now integrating the level and run information into the `HeaderStatsBar`. I\'ve calculated the level based on XP and added \"EAGLE RUN\" and the current date for context. The level uses `NES_GOLD` and run uses `NES_WHITE`. I\'m meticulously using monospace fonts and bold styles to capture the retro vibe.\n\n\n**Designing Console Control**\n\nI\'m now starting on the console\'s physical controller layout, taking visual details directly from the HTML. I\'m focusing on SELECT and START buttons first, which are circular, with text labels in an appropriate font. Now, I\'m integrating the A and B buttons. The goal is to accurately translate these visual elements into the `BottomNavBar` composable.\n\n\n**Implementing Gamepad Controls**\n\nI\'m now fully focused on the A and B buttons. The \"B\" button\'s function is now tailored for adding custom interactions based on the active tab. For example, it will open the \"FORGE CUSTOM BUFF\" dialog in the Tavern, and the \"EXTERNALISE ANXIETY\" field in the Closet. I\'m also ensuring the correct tab cycle: TAVERN -> CLOSET -> MORGUE -> TAVERN.\n\n\n**Implementing Button Visuals**\n\nI\'m now implementing the A and B buttons. The \"A\" button will complete or check off a first incomplete item. \"B\" will add interactions. The rounded buttons for SELECT and START are grey with tactile borders. For A and B, I\'m focusing on dark red shadows, created using custom layout offsets and background color drops, simulating 3D depth. I\'m focusing on lines 44-167 of `MainLayout`.\n\n\n**Refactoring Control Logic**\n\nI\'m now refactoring the `showAddHabitDialog` state. I\'m lifting it to `MainLayout` to allow direct controller button invocation. If \"B\" is pressed, I\'ll show the \"Forge custom buff\" dialog in the Tavern, or I\'ll highlight the \"What worries you?\" text field in the Closet. I\'m prioritizing direct button-to-functionality mapping.\n\n\n**Mapping Button Interactions**\n\nI\'m now integrating button callbacks for a better user experience. Button \"B\" will trigger either `triggerAddHabit` for the Tavern or `triggerAddWorry` for the Closet, both lifted to `MainLayout`. I\'m synchronizing states for habit and worry additions. My main focus is a complete gamepad for the user, identical to the visual HTML design.\n\n**Integrating Gamepad Layout**\n\nI\'m now composing a reusable `ImmersiveGamepad` composable, importing the right Jetpack Compose elements from `MainLayout.kt`. I\'ll use it to visualize and link gamepad buttons with the `MainLayout`. Specifically, I\'m integrating button callbacks for SELECT, START, A, and B, using their visual properties from the HTML.\n\n\n**Implementing Button Actions**\n\nI\'m now integrating the visual layout of the `ImmersiveGamepad` with the functionality. I\'m focusing on the \"SEL\" (SELECT) button, its appearance, and its `onSelectClick()` action. I\'m also implementing the \"START\" button, adding its visual properties and linking it to the proper function, directly calling the provided `onStartClick()` action. I\'m prioritizing the user\'s gamepad experience.\n\n\n\n**Visualizing Button Interactions**\n\nI\'m now implementing the START button\'s visual components and connecting them to its action, which is `onStartClick()`. I\'m using monospace font and bold style to the labels in NES_GREY for the \"START\" and \"SELECT\" buttons. The focus is now on connecting button appearance with functionality, ensuring tactile retro design.\n\n\n**Refining Button Rendering**\n\nI\'m now rendering the A and B buttons, aiming for the correct retro arcade style. The buttons will have dark red shadows. I am prioritizing custom layout offsets and background colors for 3D depth, exactly as the HTML describes. I\'m focusing on lines 44-167 of the HTML file, the exact section containing the code needed to render the buttons.\n\n\n**Rendering Button Effects**\n\nI\'m now implementing the dark red shadows for the A and B buttons, taking inspiration from the HTML file. I am using custom layout offsets and background colors to achieve the right 3D-like depth and tactile design. My focus is still lines 44-167 of `MainLayout`, precisely.\n\n\n\n**Refining Inventory Display**\n\nI\'m now integrating the A button and its visuals. The button triggers an inventory action, displaying the items I have. I\'ve incorporated custom layout offsets and background colors for 3D depth and tactile design as in the HTML. I\'m focusing on lines 44-167 of the HTML file, the exact section containing the code.\n\n\n**Implementing Button Effects**\n\nI\'m now rendering the \"A\" button, focusing on the visual effects, and making sure the button appears tactile. I\'m using the HTML\'s custom layout offsets and background colors for 3D depth. I am now focused on a retro style and lines 44-167 of the HTML file.\n\n\n**Implementing Sage\'s Guidance**\n\nI\'m now implementing the START button\'s action to show a \"SAGE\'S HELP SCROLL\" dialog, offering game mechanics guidance. If no check-in is due, the button will open a custom dialog with a manual. I\'m focusing on lines 44-167 of `MainLayout.kt` and the new dialog design.\n\n\n**Designing Gamepad Actions**\n\nI\'m now implementing the gamepad\'s button actions. The \"B\" button now opens the appropriate add dialog based on the current screen, toggling `showAddHabitDialog` or `showAddWorryDialog` as needed. The \"A\" button completes the first active task, whether it\'s an unchecked habit in the Tavern or an active worry in the Closet. This is pretty cool!\n\n\n"
