    thinking: "**Adding UI Element**\n\nI\'m currently focused on UI updates. I\'ve decided to add a \"\342\232\224\357\270\217 HERO CREATOR\" button next to the \"+ FORGE BUFF\" button, creating a row of buttons at the top right for UI elements. I\'m implementing it in Kotlin using a `Row` layout for button spacing.\n\n\n**Refining UI Component**\n\nI\'ve crafted the \"\342\232\224\357\270\217 HERO CREATOR\" button with a golden border and background. It\'s now clickable, sets `showCharacterCreatorDialog` to true, and includes a test tag. I\'m passing `heroSprite` to `TavernPanel`, ready for dynamic character rendering in dialogue. I\'ve placed the logic to open the dialog in the `TavernPanel` to control the data flow.\n\n\n**Integrating Sprite Data**\n\nI\'m now integrating `heroSprite` and `profileState` into `TavernPanel`. I\'ve added `heroSprite: List<List<Color>>` to the `TavernPanel` Composable. This should allow dynamic rendering of the custom sprite. I\'ve updated the dialogue box in `TavernPanel` to render the custom sprite using `PixelSprite`. I can now display the adventurer\'s sprite in the dialogue.\n\n\n**Designing Dialog Selections**\n\nI\'m now designing the state modifiers for the `CharacterCreatorDialog`. I\'m going to base the selector UI on Material 3 dialog options, aiming to create pixel border selections for a retro aesthetic. I\'ve defined modifiers for `classType`, `primaryColor`, `secondaryColor`, and `accessoryColor`. I\'m planning to create a beautifully styled options row, either using color selector circles or retro square option cards. I\'ve started the implementation with the initial composable function for the dialog.\n\n\n**Defining Color Selectors**\n\nI\'m now implementing color selectors within the `CharacterCreatorDialog`. I\'ve added state variables for `selectedSpriteType`, `selectedPrimaryColor`, `selectedSecondaryColor`, and `selectedAccessoryColor`. The `previewSprite` now dynamically updates based on these selections, using `ZeldaSprites.getCustomHeroSprite`. I\'m working on the dialog\'s structure, using a `RetroDoubleBorderContainer` and a `Column` with vertical scrolling.\n\n\n**Presenting Dialogue Elements**\n\nI\'ve added a title to the character creation dialog, \"HERO ASSEMBLY CHAMBER,\" styled with a retro font and gold color. I\'ve also implemented a large live preview of the hero sprite within a double-border container. A pixel sprite displays the currently selected character with appropriate spacing. I\'m starting to build the class selection section, with a title and ready for class options.\n\n\n**Implementing Class Options**\n\nI\'m now focusing on class selection within the dialog. I\'ve built a row of class options using \"HERO,\" \"SAGE,\" \"KNIGHT,\" and \"FAIRY.\" I\'m using `Box` containers with borders and backgrounds to visually represent the selection. Each option is clickable, updating `selectedSpriteType` and visually highlighting the selected choice. I\'m now working to ensure the selected class visually affects the live sprite preview.\n\n\n**Adding Color Selections**\n\nI\'m now implementing the primary color selection for the hero\'s outfit. I\'m focusing on creating a row of color options, each represented by a clickable `Box` displaying the NES colors. I\'m going to dynamically update a text element that\'ll show the current selections. The display will have centered text, using monospace font, bold styling, and a suitable font size, all done in a clean row layout.\n\n\n**Styling Color Selection**\n\nI\'ve just implemented the color selection logic using a `HorizontalArrangement` with spacing. I\'m dynamically updating a text display to show the current primary selection, with a monospace font and bold styling. Each color option is represented by a clickable `Box` displaying the NES color palette. I\'ve created visual feedback for the user by updating the border based on the selected colors.\n\n\n**Refining UI Colors**\n\nI\'m now refining the color selection UI for the character creator. I\'m focusing on the secondary outfit color selection, implementing it with a similar row of color options, each a clickable box. The row is visually clear and easy to select from, while a textual representation of the current selection will be displayed. I\'ve ensured seamless updates to the preview sprite with color selections.\n\n\n**Implementing Accessory Selection**\n\nI\'m now implementing the accessory color selection, creating another row with clickable color options. I\'m dynamically updating a text element to reflect the current accessory color selection, with a monospace font and bold styling. I\'ve designed the UI to seamlessly update the live sprite preview.\n\n\n**Enhancing Visual Feedback**\n\nI\'m enhancing the visual feedback for color selections, specifically for the secondary color. I\'m building on the previous color selection implementation to refine the user experience. The color options are clickable boxes, visually distinct with borders and backgrounds. Currently, I\'m working on the logic, creating seamless and responsive UI updates.\n\n\n**Finalizing Sprite Colors**\n\nI\'m now putting the finishing touches on the color selection interface. I\'ve built and styled a row of accessory/hair color options. Each option is a clickable box that updates the accessory color selection. The UI dynamically adjusts the text, style, and border to showcase the selected accessory color. The goal is a highly refined and responsive experience for the user.\n\n\n**Finalizing Dialog Interaction**\n\nI\'m now putting the finishing touches on the character creation dialog interaction and responsiveness. I\'ve successfully built the accessory/hair color options with dynamic feedback. Each option is a clickable box which updates the accessory color selection, giving visual feedback. I\'m focused on a refined experience for the user. I\'m almost ready for implementing the final action!\n\n\n**Adding Action Buttons**\n\nI\'ve just added action buttons to the dialog. I\'ve included an \"ABORT\" button using `TextButton`, colored NES_GREY, and a \"SAVE\" button using a `Button`. The \"SAVE\" button triggers `onSave` to record the selected sprite. I\'m ensuring both action buttons are styled with the monospace font. I\'m making sure they are functional!\n\n\n\n**Verifying Dependencies**\n\nI\'m verifying the imports for `verticalScroll` and `rememberScrollState` to ensure the character creation dialog functions correctly. After checking `MainLayout.kt`, I\'ve confirmed that these dependencies are properly imported, allowing smooth scrolling. Now, I\'m integrating the \'EQUIP HERO\' button functionality.\n\n\n"
